{"name":"Mesquite Powder","hex":"#e3c8b1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mesquite","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several deciduous trees of the genus Prosopis found in America, and used as forage, which have long, beige seed and bean pods which may be dried and ground into a sweet, nutty flour.","The wood of these trees, used for smoking food, or charcoal made from this wood."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mesquite"},{"term":"powder","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The fine particles which are the result of reducing a dry substance by pounding, grinding, or triturating, or the result of decay; dust.\n\n Cocaine in powder form (as opposed to crack).","Cocaine in powder form (as opposed to crack)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To reduce to fine particles; to pound, grind, or rub into a powder.","To sprinkle with powder, or as if with powder."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/powder"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mesquite flour","blocks":[{"text":"Mesquite flour is made from the dried and ground pods of the mesquite, a tree that grows throughout Mexico and the southwestern US in arid and drought-prone climates. The flour made from the long, beige-colored seedpods has a sweet, slightly nutty flavor and can be used in a wide variety of applications. It has a high-protein, low-glycemic content and can serve as a gluten-free replacement for flours that contain gluten."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Starr_021217-9001_Prosopis_pallida.jpg/330px-Starr_021217-9001_Prosopis_pallida.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mesquite_flour","wikidata":"Q6821694"},{"title":"Mesquite","blocks":[{"text":"Mesquite is a common name for some plants in the genera Neltuma and Strombocarpa, which contain over 50 species of spiny, deep-rooted leguminous shrubs and small trees. They are native to dry areas in the Americas. Until 2022, these genera were traditionally included in a broad view of the genus Prosopis, but that genus is now restricted to a few species native to the Old World."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Mezquites_en_Jes%C3%BAs_Mar%C3%ADa%2C_Aguascalientes_08.jpg/330px-Mezquites_en_Jes%C3%BAs_Mar%C3%ADa%2C_Aguascalientes_08.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mesquite","wikidata":"Q3315767"},{"title":"Powder","blocks":[{"text":"A powder is a dry solid composed of many very fine particles that may flow freely when shaken or tilted. Powders are a special sub-class of granular materials, although the terms powder and granular are sometimes used to distinguish separate classes of material. In particular, powders refer to those granular materials that have the finer grain sizes, and that therefore have a greater tendency to form clumps when flowing. Granulars refer to the coarser granular materials that do not tend to form clumps except when wet."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Iron_powder.JPG/330px-Iron_powder.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Powder","wikidata":"Q2908004"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Mesquite_Range_in_the_United_States.jpg/500px-Mesquite_Range_in_the_United_States.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Mesquite_Range_in_the_United_States.jpg/1280px-Mesquite_Range_in_the_United_States.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Mesquite_Range_in_the_United_States.jpg/500px-Mesquite_Range_in_the_United_States.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Nonfederal  rangeland where aggressive, native mesquite species are present in the United States","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMesquite_Range_in_the_United_States.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Prosopis_pubescens_beans.jpg/120px-Prosopis_pubescens_beans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Prosopis_pubescens_beans.jpg/250px-Prosopis_pubescens_beans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Prosopis_pubescens_beans.jpg/120px-Prosopis_pubescens_beans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AProsopis_pubescens_beans.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Prosopis_pubescens_inflorescence_2003-06-02.jpg/120px-Prosopis_pubescens_inflorescence_2003-06-02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Prosopis_pubescens_inflorescence_2003-06-02.jpg/250px-Prosopis_pubescens_inflorescence_2003-06-02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Prosopis_pubescens_inflorescence_2003-06-02.jpg/120px-Prosopis_pubescens_inflorescence_2003-06-02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AProsopis_pubescens_inflorescence_2003-06-02.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Prosopis-glandulosa-seed-pods.jpg/120px-Prosopis-glandulosa-seed-pods.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Prosopis-glandu